@page "/admin"
@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Admin.Components
@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Admin.ViewModels
@inherits MvvmComponentBase<AdminViewModel>

@*
    The prototype's compliance, security and audit screen: heading with the ten tabs,
    then the pane.

    Staff, sites, registrations, the audit trail, the local database and the mail settings
    are real. Roles, rosters and licensing are not, and this is the screen where saying so
    matters most — a practice manager reading "MFA enforced" or "Reception: read-only"
    would take a control as configured when none exists.

    Every write on these panes records an audit entry, which is what gives the audit log
    real content to show.
*@

<PageTitle>Admin — Molargo</PageTitle>

<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-5 border-b-2 border-divider px-6 py-3">
    <h4 class="m-0">Admin</h4>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
        @foreach (var tab in Tabs)
        {
            <button class="@TabCss(tab.Value)"
                    @onclick="() => ViewModel.SelectTabCommand.ExecuteAsync(tab.Value)">
                @tab.Label
            </button>
        }
    </div>

    @*
        The design's "Signed in as Owner · all actions below are themselves audit-logged".
        Both halves are now true: there is a real sign-in, and every write on these panes
        writes an audit entry.
    *@
    <span class="ml-auto text-xs text-ink/55">
        Signed in as @ViewModel.ActingAs · actions here are audited
    </span>
</div>

@*
    A failure wins over the last success rather than stacking under it: a refused save
    does not clear the previous action's confirmation, and the two together read as
    though both had happened.
*@
@if (ViewModel.HasError)
{
    <NoticeComponent Kind="NoticeKind.Failure"
                     Headline="@ViewModel.ErrorHeadline"
                     Message="@ViewModel.ErrorMessage" />
}
else if (ViewModel.LastAction is { Length: > 0 } action)
{
    <NoticeComponent Message="@action" />
}

@switch (ViewModel.Tab)
{
    case AdminTab.Users:
        <StaffComponent Vm="ViewModel" />
        break;

    case AdminTab.Roles:
        <RolesComponent Vm="ViewModel" />
        break;

    case AdminTab.Sites:
        <SitesComponent Vm="ViewModel" />
        break;

    case AdminTab.Registrations:
        <RegistrationsComponent Vm="ViewModel" />
        break;

    case AdminTab.Audit:
        <AuditLogComponent Vm="ViewModel" />
        break;

    case AdminTab.Settings:
        <SettingsComponent Vm="ViewModel" />
        break;

    case AdminTab.Data:
        <DataComponent Vm="ViewModel" />
        break;

    case AdminTab.Security:
        @*
            Rewritten once sign-in existed. This pane used to open by saying there was no
            authentication at all, which was true when it was written and is now the
            opposite of true — a stale disclosure is worse than none, because a practice
            reads it and stops trusting the parts that do work.
        *@
        <div class="max-w-[760px] px-6 py-6">
            <h5 class="mt-0">Security</h5>

            <div class="mb-4 border-2 border-divider bg-surface p-3 text-sm">
                <strong>What holds today</strong>
                <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink/70">
                    ✓ <strong>Sign-in</strong> — clinic code, username and password, with
                    the password stored as a PBKDF2-SHA256 hash at 210,000 iterations and a
                    per-user salt. Never in the clear.<br />
                    ✓ <strong>Lockout</strong> — five wrong passwords locks an account for
                    fifteen minutes.<br />
                    ✓ <strong>One message for every failure</strong>, so the screen cannot
                    be used to discover which clinics exist or who works at them.<br />
                    ✓ <strong>Sessions</strong> — an HttpOnly cookie, eight hours sliding,
                    on the web head. Page script cannot read it.<br />
                    ✓ <strong>Sign-in and failed attempts are audited</strong>, with the
                    installation they came from.<br />
                    ✓ <strong>Tenant isolation</strong> — every table is filtered by clinic
                    at the database level, so one practice cannot read another's records
                    even with a record's id.
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="mb-4 border-2 border-accent bg-accent-100 p-3 text-sm text-accent-800"
                 role="alert">
                <strong>The database is not encrypted.</strong>
                Records are in a plain SQLite file, and so is the mail app password on the
                Settings tab. A password gates the <em>app</em>; it does not protect the
                file. Anyone with the machine, or a copy of that folder, can read
                everything — so full-disk encryption, a screen lock and physical access you
                trust are still the controls that matter.
            </div>

            <h6 class="mt-4 mb-1">Still missing</h6>
            <div class="text-xs text-ink/55">
                ✗ <strong>MFA</strong> — needs an authenticator or passkey enrolment per
                user. The design shows it enforced; nothing enrols one.<br />
                ✗ <strong>SSO</strong> — a SAML or OIDC integration with the practice's
                identity provider, plus deprovisioning on offboarding.<br />
                ✗ <strong>Password reset</strong> — a forgotten password has to be changed
                by editing the staff record. There is no self-service reset and no
                change-password screen.<br />
                ✗ <strong>Roles that restrict access</strong> — a role decides what someone
                can be recorded as doing, not what they can open. Every signed-in user sees
                every screen.<br />
                ✗ <strong>Trusted devices</strong> — the installation id under
                <button type="button"
                        class="cursor-pointer border-0 bg-transparent p-0 font-[inherit] text-[inherit] text-ink underline hover:text-accent"
                        @onclick="() => ViewModel.SelectTabCommand.ExecuteAsync(AdminTab.Data)">Data</button>
                identifies this install and is stamped on audit entries, but nothing
                authorises or revokes a device.
            </div>
        </div>
        break;

    case AdminTab.Hr:
        @*
            Timesheets, rosters, leave and provider pay. Four entities, none of which
            exist — and the pay figures in the design are derived from production and
            collections the Reports screen can already produce, which makes this the
            closest of the unbuilt panes to being buildable.
        *@
        <div class="max-w-[760px] px-6 py-6">
            <h5 class="mt-0">Rosters, timesheets &amp; pay</h5>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/55">
                Not built. This pane needs four things the app has no record of: a roster
                (who is on, where, when), timesheets against it, leave balances, and a pay
                arrangement per provider — a percentage of production, a percentage of
                collections, or hourly with a bonus over target.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/55">
                The production and collections halves already exist:
                <button type="button"
                        class="cursor-pointer border-0 bg-transparent p-0 font-[inherit] text-[inherit] text-ink underline hover:text-accent"
                        @onclick="() => ViewModel.SelectTabCommand.ExecuteAsync(AdminTab.Users)">staff</button>
                are recorded here and the Reports screen totals each provider's production
                and the practice's collections for any period. What is missing is the
                arrangement to apply to them, and the hours worked to check it against.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/55">
                Payroll export — Xero Payroll, KeyPay — is an integration on top of all
                four, and is online work.
            </p>
        </div>
        break;

    default:
        @*
            Subscription, seats and billing. Entirely a vendor-side concern: this app has
            no licence server, no seat count and no payment method, and it would not know
            what plan a practice is on.
        *@
        <div class="max-w-[760px] px-6 py-6">
            <h5 class="mt-0">Plan &amp; licensing</h5>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/55">
                Not built, and not local. A plan, a seat count and subscription invoices
                live with whoever sells the software — the app would have to ask a licence
                server what the practice is entitled to, and there is no server.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/55">
                Molargo currently enforces no limits at all: there is no cap on staff,
                patients or locations, and nothing meters SMS because nothing sends any.
                @ViewModel.ActiveStaffCount active
                @(ViewModel.ActiveStaffCount == 1 ? "person is" : "people are") set up on
                the <button type="button"
                        class="cursor-pointer border-0 bg-transparent p-0 font-[inherit] text-[inherit] text-ink underline hover:text-accent"
                        @onclick="() => ViewModel.SelectTabCommand.ExecuteAsync(AdminTab.Users)">Users</button>
                tab, which is a count of staff rather than of seats.
            </p>
        </div>
        break;
}

<div class="px-6 pb-6 font-mono text-[11px] text-neutral-700">
    WEB · ADMIN
</div>

@code {
    private sealed record TabDefinition(string Label, AdminTab Value);

    /// <summary>The prototype's panes, in its order.</summary>
    private static readonly TabDefinition[] Tabs =
    [
        new("Users", AdminTab.Users),
        new("Roles", AdminTab.Roles),
        new("Sites", AdminTab.Sites),
        new("Registrations", AdminTab.Registrations),
        new("Rosters & pay", AdminTab.Hr),
        new("Plan", AdminTab.Licensing),
        new("Audit log", AdminTab.Audit),
        new("Security", AdminTab.Security),
        new("Data", AdminTab.Data),
        new("Settings", AdminTab.Settings),
    ];

    private string TabCss(AdminTab tab) =>
        ViewModel.Tab == tab
            ? "-mb-0.5 cursor-pointer border-0 border-b-[3px] border-accent bg-transparent px-0 py-3 font-[inherit] text-[13px] font-bold text-ink"
            : "-mb-0.5 cursor-pointer border-0 border-b-[3px] border-transparent bg-transparent px-0 py-3 font-[inherit] text-[13px] text-ink hover:text-accent";
}
